    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d677">

                <pb facs="0574" n="458" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg458"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/11988.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Admiral Fletcher to the
                            Secretary of the Navy.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Florida. <lb/> Vera
                            Cruz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1914-04-14T15:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 14, 1914, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In reply to Department’s April 13, have received following from Mayo: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>No intimation received of existence of martial law or of objection to
                            boat taking supplies. Stewards, orderlies, etc., are going ashore when
                            necessary. <pb facs="0575" n="459" xml:id="pg459"/> Only part of boat’s
                            crew out of boat to handle stores. Men were sailors, not marines.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayo</hi>.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fletcher</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
